1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,606
Two cyclists leave the same gate.

2
00:00:02,606 --> 00:00:04,762
Both end up five kilometres away.

3
00:00:04,762 --> 00:00:06,632
Are they in the same place?

4
00:00:07,032 --> 00:00:07,994
Not always.

5
00:00:07,994 --> 00:00:11,057
One went north east, the other south west.

6
00:00:11,057 --> 00:00:12,771
Distance is not enough.

7
00:00:12,771 --> 00:00:14,705
You also need a direction.

8
00:00:15,155 --> 00:00:17,755
Aarav thought about
this on his ride today.

9
00:00:18,105 --> 00:00:21,666
He rode four kilometres east,
then three kilometres north.

10
00:00:21,666 --> 00:00:24,814
How far is he from home,
and in which direction?

11
00:00:25,214 --> 00:00:27,443
A complex number answers both.

12
00:00:27,443 --> 00:00:29,291
How far is the modulus.

13
00:00:29,291 --> 00:00:31,302
Which way is the argument.

14
00:00:31,952 --> 00:00:33,126
First, the picture.

15
00:00:33,476 --> 00:00:38,503
Think of z, a plus i b, as a trip
from the origin O: a steps along the

16
00:00:38,503 --> 00:00:41,766
real axis, then b steps
up, to the point P.

17
00:00:42,166 --> 00:00:44,901
Episode forty three
builds this map in full.

18
00:00:44,901 --> 00:00:46,768
Today, we measure the trip.

19
00:00:47,368 --> 00:00:48,268
How far?

20
00:00:48,146 --> 00:00:52,619
That is the modulus of z, written
with two straight bars around z.

21
00:00:52,969 --> 00:00:53,869
Step one.

22
00:00:53,779 --> 00:00:56,368
The trip makes a right angled triangle.

23
00:00:56,368 --> 00:00:58,720
The base is a, the height is b.

24
00:00:59,470 --> 00:01:00,370
Step two.

25
00:01:00,359 --> 00:01:04,597
Pythagoras says OP squared
equals a squared plus b squared.

26
00:01:05,347 --> 00:01:06,271
Step three.

27
00:01:06,271 --> 00:01:07,818
Take the square root.

28
00:01:07,818 --> 00:01:12,126
The modulus of z is the square
root of a squared plus b squared.

29
00:01:12,876 --> 00:01:16,482
For Aarav: four squared plus
three squared is twenty five.

30
00:01:16,482 --> 00:01:18,121
Its square root is five.

31
00:01:18,121 --> 00:01:20,192
He is five kilometres from home.

32
00:01:20,842 --> 00:01:26,106
And recall: z times z bar is a squared
plus b squared, which is the modulus,

33
00:01:26,106 --> 00:01:27,006
squared.

34
00:01:27,437 --> 00:01:28,705
Now, which way?

35
00:01:28,705 --> 00:01:30,598
That is the argument of z.

36
00:01:30,948 --> 00:01:35,983
The argument is the angle from the
positive real axis to the line OP.

37
00:01:35,983 --> 00:01:38,300
Anticlockwise counts as positive.

38
00:01:38,700 --> 00:01:40,663
Clockwise counts as negative.

39
00:01:40,663 --> 00:01:44,061
We pick the one angle
above minus pi, up to pi.

40
00:01:44,061 --> 00:01:46,185
That is the principal argument.

41
00:01:46,585 --> 00:01:47,882
Watch a trip run.

42
00:01:47,882 --> 00:01:49,672
z moves around the origin.

43
00:01:49,672 --> 00:01:52,208
Its distance and its angle update live.

44
00:01:52,608 --> 00:01:55,779
Below the real axis, the
angle turns negative.

45
00:01:55,779 --> 00:02:00,672
And z bar, the mirror, keeps the same
modulus but the opposite argument.

46
00:02:01,072 --> 00:02:04,059
Zero is the only number with no argument.

47
00:02:04,059 --> 00:02:06,166
It has no direction at all.

48
00:02:06,816 --> 00:02:07,716
Quick one.

49
00:02:07,708 --> 00:02:12,244
One plus i, and minus one minus
i: same argument, or different?

50
00:02:12,244 --> 00:02:13,507
Pause and think.

51
00:02:16,707 --> 00:02:17,607
Different!

52
00:02:17,519 --> 00:02:21,348
b over a is one for both,
but they point opposite ways.

53
00:02:21,348 --> 00:02:24,194
Pi by four, and minus three pi by four.

54
00:02:24,844 --> 00:02:28,112
So how do we find the
argument without a protractor?

55
00:02:28,462 --> 00:02:29,362
Step one.

56
00:02:29,298 --> 00:02:34,116
Find the reference angle, alpha: the
sharp angle whose tangent is the size

57
00:02:34,116 --> 00:02:35,226
of b over a.

58
00:02:35,976 --> 00:02:36,876
Step two.

59
00:02:36,817 --> 00:02:40,109
Use the signs of a and
b to find the quadrant.

60
00:02:40,859 --> 00:02:41,840
Step three.

61
00:02:41,840 --> 00:02:44,185
First quadrant, theta is alpha.

62
00:02:44,185 --> 00:02:46,584
Second quadrant, pi minus alpha.

63
00:02:47,334 --> 00:02:49,884
Third quadrant, minus pi plus alpha.

64
00:02:49,884 --> 00:02:51,957
Fourth quadrant, minus alpha.

65
00:02:52,707 --> 00:02:55,484
For Aarav, tan alpha is three over four.

66
00:02:55,484 --> 00:02:58,860
Alpha is about thirty seven
degrees, north of east.

67
00:02:59,460 --> 00:03:00,646
Side by side.

68
00:03:00,646 --> 00:03:05,279
Three plus four i, and three
minus four i, are mirror images.

69
00:03:05,279 --> 00:03:06,904
Same modulus, five.

70
00:03:06,904 --> 00:03:08,353
Opposite arguments.

71
00:03:08,753 --> 00:03:13,567
Three plus four i, and minus three
minus four i, also share the modulus

72
00:03:13,567 --> 00:03:16,120
five, but they point opposite ways.

73
00:03:16,720 --> 00:03:17,878
Now you try.

74
00:03:17,878 --> 00:03:21,478
Is the modulus of three
minus four i also five?

75
00:03:21,478 --> 00:03:22,917
Pause and decide.

76
00:03:26,117 --> 00:03:27,017
Yes!

77
00:03:26,622 --> 00:03:30,078
Square both parts: nine
plus sixteen is twenty five.

78
00:03:30,078 --> 00:03:32,929
A minus sign disappears
when you square it.

79
00:03:33,579 --> 00:03:34,643
Remember this.

80
00:03:34,643 --> 00:03:36,741
The modulus tells you how far.

81
00:03:36,741 --> 00:03:38,988
The argument tells you which way.

82
00:03:39,688 --> 00:03:41,423
Now, one worked example.

83
00:03:41,773 --> 00:03:46,471
Find the modulus and the principal
argument of minus one, minus root three

84
00:03:46,471 --> 00:03:47,371
i.

85
00:03:47,387 --> 00:03:48,287
Step one.

86
00:03:48,192 --> 00:03:52,394
The modulus is the square root
of one plus three, which is two.

87
00:03:53,144 --> 00:03:54,044
Step two.

88
00:03:53,917 --> 00:03:57,718
tan alpha is root three over
one, so alpha is pi by three.

89
00:03:58,468 --> 00:03:59,368
Step three.

90
00:03:59,299 --> 00:04:02,894
a and b are both negative, so
z is in the third quadrant.

91
00:04:03,644 --> 00:04:04,608
Step four.

92
00:04:04,608 --> 00:04:09,705
Theta is minus pi plus pi by three,
which is minus two pi by three.

93
00:04:10,455 --> 00:04:15,567
So the modulus is two, and the principal
argument is minus two pi by three.

94
00:04:16,217 --> 00:04:17,682
Now, the exam trap.

95
00:04:18,032 --> 00:04:20,868
Find the argument of minus one minus i.

96
00:04:20,868 --> 00:04:25,175
Many students take tan inverse
of one, and write pi by four.

97
00:04:25,575 --> 00:04:28,677
But pi by four points
into the first quadrant.

98
00:04:28,677 --> 00:04:31,217
Minus one minus i sits in the third.

99
00:04:31,217 --> 00:04:34,169
The right answer is
minus three pi by four.

100
00:04:34,819 --> 00:04:36,517
Where is this asked?

101
00:04:36,517 --> 00:04:41,907
In JEE Main and JEE Advanced, on the
modulus and the principal argument.

102
00:04:41,907 --> 00:04:45,074
And in PUC board exams, on the modulus.

103
00:04:45,674 --> 00:04:46,964
Exam level now.

104
00:04:46,964 --> 00:04:50,506
Find the modulus and the
principal argument of z.

105
00:04:50,506 --> 00:04:51,850
Pause and solve.

106
00:04:57,250 --> 00:04:58,592
The answer is A.

107
00:04:58,592 --> 00:05:01,005
Root two, and three pi by four.

108
00:05:01,505 --> 00:05:04,201
First, one plus i, squared, is two i.

109
00:05:04,701 --> 00:05:08,528
Multiply top and bottom by
the conjugate, one plus i.

110
00:05:08,528 --> 00:05:12,525
The bottom becomes two, and
z becomes minus one plus i.

111
00:05:13,025 --> 00:05:14,793
The modulus is root two.

112
00:05:14,793 --> 00:05:19,703
z is in the second quadrant with alpha
pi by four, so the argument is three

113
00:05:19,703 --> 00:05:20,693
pi by four.

114
00:05:21,293 --> 00:05:22,752
Here is your recap.

115
00:05:22,752 --> 00:05:24,252
Screenshot this card.

116
00:05:27,452 --> 00:05:30,450
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

117
00:05:30,850 --> 00:05:31,750
One.

118
00:05:31,406 --> 00:05:34,948
What is the modulus of
minus five plus twelve i?

119
00:05:35,848 --> 00:05:36,748
Two.

120
00:05:36,421 --> 00:05:40,241
What is the principal
argument of minus one plus i?

121
00:05:41,141 --> 00:05:42,041
Three.

122
00:05:41,849 --> 00:05:45,693
Why do z and z bar always
have the same modulus?

123
00:05:46,793 --> 00:05:51,305
Next time: Diya gives every complex
number its own address on a map.

124
00:05:51,305 --> 00:05:53,356
That map is the Argand plane.
